
//================================================================
// procedure MemoryInterface
// Author: Neha Karanjkar
//
// A minimal, blocking memory-access interface, called synchronously
// (via `run`) by SparcThread's ifetch/load/store/atomic/flush procedures --
// see SparcThread.sitar for why these are procedure calls and not
// module/port connections. Each `run` performs exactly one access and
// returns; the procedure instance itself is not destroyed and is reused
// for the next access (no loop of its own -- contrast with whatever's
// downstream, MainMemoryVA or MainMemory depending on config, which is
// persistent).
//
// `request`/`response` (VirtualMemoryRequest/VirtualMemoryResponse, Ref
// MemoryInterfaces.h) are this procedure's own public state: SparcThread
// sets request.address/asi/word0/word1/byteMask directly before calling
// `run`, and reads response.readWord0/readWord1/mae afterwards -- the
// same struct types and field names used everywhere else memory
// requests/responses are described (cpp_model's MemCore/MainMemory/Mmu,
// and this file's own downstream connection below), not a parallel,
// independently-named shadow copy of them.
//
// Talks to whatever persistent procedure is connected on the other end
// (MainMemoryVA for core_only, MainMemory for core_mmu; later, a cache)
// through `downstreamRequest`/`downstreamResponse` -- pointers directly
// into that downstream procedure's OWN local request/response fields
// (e.g. mainMemory.request/mainMemory.response), not a separate object owned
// by this procedure, this file's downstream neighbor, or the parent
// module. The parent module (see Core.sitar) only wires these pointers
// together at init
// (e.g. `ifetchProcedure.downstreamRequest = &mainMemory.request;`); it
// does not own or hold a copy of the state itself. This is the general
// chaining pattern for a run of persistent procedures A -> B -> C
// (arrows = request flow): each procedure but the first owns its own
// local request/response (the object its upstream neighbor reads/
// writes), and each procedure but the last holds downstream pointers
// into the *next* procedure's own local request/response -- so a chain
// of any length is just a chain of identically-shaped links, with no
// extra shared objects introduced at the parent level. Analogous to how
// AJIT's own sysMemBusRequest() hands a request to whatever services the
// bus. Because both sides are procedures running as branches of the same
// parallel block -- not modules connected by nets -- this handshake can
// complete within a single phase, i.e. with 0 added cycles, exactly like
// PipelinedProcessor's inter-stage handshake in the sitar repo's
// examples. If more than one requester ever needs to reach this same
// memory (e.g. before ifetch and load/store are split onto separate
// caches), point their downstreamRequest/downstreamResponse pointers at
// that same downstream procedure's local request/response -- only one is
// ever in flight at a time under SparcThread's current, strictly
// sequential FSM.
//
// NOTE on `valid`: `request`/`response` and `*downstreamRequest`/
// `*downstreamResponse` are two DIFFERENT handshakes, upstream-facing and
// downstream-facing respectively, and each one's `valid` bit is owned by
// a single side of that handshake -- downstreamRequest->valid is set by
// this procedure and cleared by this procedure once the response is
// consumed; downstreamResponse->valid is set by whatever services it
// (MainMemoryVA or MainMemory) and cleared by this procedure. request.valid/
// response.valid are the same shape one level up: they would be set by
// whoever calls this procedure and cleared by this procedure (request),
// and set by this procedure and cleared by the caller (response) -- Ref
// below, only relevant once a persistent, chained procedure connects
// upstream of this one via those fields rather than SparcThread's direct
// field-set-then-`run` calling convention.
//
// Because of this, the payload fields (address/asi/word0/word1/byteMask/
// readWord0/readWord1/mae) are copied individually between `request`/
// `response` and `*downstreamRequest`/`*downstreamResponse` below --
// never via a whole-struct assignment -- so that each `valid` bit is only
// ever touched by the code that actually owns it under the handshake it
// belongs to. Under today's calling convention (SparcThread sets fields
// directly and calls `run` synchronously, blocking until it returns) the
// local request.valid/response.valid are simply never touched by this
// procedure and stay at their init value -- correct today, and already
// in the right shape for a future persistent, chained procedure (e.g. a
// cache) that DOES need those fields to carry real upstream handshake
// state, with no rework needed here when that happens.
//
// NOTE:
//	all accesses are double word accesses.
//	address must be double word aligned.
//	For writes: a byte mask is used to write to a specific set of
//	bytes in the double word.
//================================================================


procedure MemoryInterface

	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//Fields to be set by processor at start:
	//--------------------------------------------------------
	decl
	$
	//processor and core IDS to be set by parent module at start
	uint32_t processorID;
	uint32_t coreID;

	//which kind of access this instance performs -- fixed for the
	//lifetime of the instance (e.g. SparcThread's memReadProcedure is
	//always LOAD), set once by the parent module's init.
	MemAccessType memoryAccessType;

	//total number of requests made by this procedure
	uint64_t requestCount;
	$
	init
	$
		processorID=0;
		coreID=0;
		memoryAccessType = MemAccessType::NO_ACCESS;
		requestCount = 0;
	$

	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//Access latency, modeled on the core side of this interface.
	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//
	//Cycles only (not phases): the number of *extra* cycles this
	//procedure blocks its caller for, beyond the 0-delay procedure-call
	//semantics it would otherwise have. Default 0. Lets a directly
	//connected MainMemoryVA/MainMemory (or, later, a cache) have an access latency
	//approximated here without that latency needing to be modeled by the
	//connected procedure itself. Independent of, and in addition to, the
	//opcode-wise per-instruction delay applied in SparcThread (see
	//OpcodeLatencies.h).
	decl$uint32_t delay;$
	init$delay = 0;$
	//--------------------------------------------------------



	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//This procedure's own public request/response state -- Ref this
	//file's own header comment. SparcThread.sitar sets the request
	//fields directly (request.address, request.asi, request.word0,
	//request.word1, request.byteMask) before calling `run`, and reads
	//the response fields (response.readWord0, response.readWord1,
	//response.mae) afterwards.
	//--------------------------------------------------------
	include
	$
	#include"MemoryInterfaces.h"
	$

	decl
	$
	VirtualMemoryRequest  request;
	VirtualMemoryResponse response;
	$
	init
	$
	request.valid      = false;
	request.accessType = MemAccessType::NO_ACCESS;
	request.address    = 0;
	request.asi        = 0;
	request.word0      = 0;
	request.word1      = 0;
	request.byteMask   = 0;

	response.valid     = false;
	response.readWord0 = 0;
	response.readWord1 = 0;
	response.mae       = false;
	$
	//--------------------------------------------------------



	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//Stats collection
	//--------------------------------------------------------
	decl
	$
	long unsigned int numAccesses;  //total number of mem accesses finished
	long double 	  totalLatency; //sum of latencies of all accesses (cycles)

	//start time of the access currently in flight
	long unsigned int startTime;
	$
	init
	$
	numAccesses=0;
	totalLatency=0;
	$
	//--------------------------------------------------------



	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//Connection to whatever is servicing this interface (MainMemoryVA or
	//MainMemory, or later a cache) -- pointers directly into that downstream
	//procedure's own local request/response fields, wired up by the
	//parent module at init (Ref this file's header comment).
	//--------------------------------------------------------

	decl
	$
	VirtualMemoryRequest*  downstreamRequest;
	VirtualMemoryResponse* downstreamResponse;
	$
	init
	$
	downstreamRequest  = NULL;
	downstreamResponse = NULL;
	$
	//--------------------------------------------------------


	//--------------------------------------------------------
	//Behavior
	//--------------------------------------------------------
behavior

	//check that this instance has been initialized by the parent module
	//correctly before making any request
	$
	assert(memoryAccessType!=MemAccessType::NO_ACCESS);
	assert(downstreamRequest);
	assert(downstreamResponse);
	$;

	//wait until whatever is servicing this interface is free, then issue
	//the request
	wait until (this_phase==0 and not($downstreamRequest->valid$));

	$
	startTime = current_time.cycle();
	log<<endl<<"[t="<<current_time<<"] Started memory reference of type "<<accessTypeToString(memoryAccessType)
	   <<" for address 0x"<<std::hex<<request.address<<std::dec;

	//Individual field writes onto the *shared* downstream object -- not
	//an aggregate copy of `request` (Ref this file's header comment on
	//`valid`). downstreamRequest->valid is set last and explicitly: it
	//is this procedure's own handshake flag on the downstream side, not
	//a payload field being relayed from `request`.
	downstreamRequest->accessType = memoryAccessType;
	downstreamRequest->address    = request.address;
	downstreamRequest->asi        = request.asi;
	downstreamRequest->word0      = request.word0;
	downstreamRequest->word1      = request.word1;
	downstreamRequest->byteMask   = request.byteMask;
	downstreamRequest->valid      = true;
	$;

	//wait for the response (the access_type check is a cheap consistency
	//check)
	wait until (this_phase==0 and $downstreamResponse->valid and downstreamRequest->accessType==memoryAccessType$);

	$
	//Individual field reads off the *shared* downstream object -- again
	//not an aggregate copy. response.valid is deliberately left
	//untouched here: it belongs to a different handshake (Ref header
	//comment), not to downstreamResponse->valid.
	response.readWord0 = downstreamResponse->readWord0;
	response.readWord1 = downstreamResponse->readWord1;
	response.mae        = downstreamResponse->mae;

	//close the transaction: explicit, individual field writes on the
	//*shared* objects -- frees the responder to service the next request,
	//and frees this (or another) interface to issue one.
	downstreamRequest->valid  = false;
	downstreamResponse->valid = false;
	$;

	//model the configured access latency (default 0 -- see `delay` above)
	if ($delay > 0$) then
		wait(delay, 0);
	end if;

	$
	requestCount++;
	numAccesses++;
	totalLatency += double(current_time.cycle() - startTime);

	log<<endl<<"[t="<<current_time<<"] Finished memory reference of type "<<accessTypeToString(memoryAccessType)
	   <<" for address 0x"<<std::hex<<request.address<<std::dec;
	if(memoryAccessType==MemAccessType::IFETCH) log<<"  Fetched instruction word 0x"<<std::hex<<response.readWord0<<std::dec;
	$;


	nothing;

end behavior
end procedure

